          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In this exercise we’ll explore a clever trick for multiplying two numbers that are each a little over 100. We’ll look at two examples first and then see why it works. Consider",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p6-q14-demonstration1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "107\\times109=1\\;\\overbrace{16}^{7+9}\\;\\overbrace{63}^{7\\times9}",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p6-q14-demonstration2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "102\\times103=1\\;\\overbrace{05}^{2+3}\\;\\overbrace{06}^{2\\times3}",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p6-q14-a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use the shortcut above to evaluate each of the following:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$104\\times103$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$10\\,712$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}104\\times103&=10\\,000+100(4+3)+4\\times3\\\\104\\times103&=10\\,000+700+12\\\\104\\times103&=10\\,712\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "$106\\times105$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$11\\,130$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}106\\times105&=10\\,000+100(6+5)+6\\times5\\\\106\\times105&=10\\,000+1100+30\\\\106\\times105&=11\\,130\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-a-iii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "$109\\times108$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$11\\,772$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}109\\times108&=10\\,000+100(9+8)+9\\times8\\\\109\\times108&=10\\,000+1700+72\\\\109\\times108&=11\\,772\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-a-iv",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iv",
            "prompt": "$(107)^2$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$11\\,449$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}107^2&=10\\,000+100(7+7)+7\\times7\\\\107^2&=10\\,000+1400+49\\\\107^2&=11\\,449\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $$(100+x)(100+y)=10\\,000+100(x+y)+xy,$$ and use this to explain how the trick works.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$(100+x)(100+y)=10\\,000+100(x+y)+xy.$$\n\nAdd the excesses for the middle two digits and multiply them for the final two digits, using a leading zero where needed and carrying if necessary.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(100+x)(100+y)&=10\\,000+100x+100y+xy\\\\(100+x)(100+y)&=10\\,000+100(x+y)+xy\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe $10\\,000$ supplies the initial 1. The sum $x+y$ supplies the next two digits and the product $xy$ supplies the final two digits. Write each group with two digits, including leading zeros; carry between groups if necessary.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find a shortcut for multiplying two numbers that are both a little over 200.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$(200+x)(200+y)=40\\,000+100\\bigl[2(x+y)\\bigr]+xy.$$\n\nStart with 4, use twice the sum of the excesses for the middle two digits, and their product for the final two digits. Use leading zeros and carry if necessary.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(200+x)(200+y)&=40\\,000+200x+200y+xy\\\\(200+x)(200+y)&=40\\,000+200(x+y)+xy\\\\(200+x)(200+y)&=40\\,000+100\\bigl[2(x+y)\\bigr]+xy\\end{align*}$$\n\nStart with 4, followed by the two-digit group $2(x+y)$ and the two-digit group $xy$. Include leading zeros and carry between groups if necessary.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q14-d",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that your shortcut works by evaluating each of the following:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-d-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$204\\times203$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$41\\,412$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}204\\times203&=40\\,000+100\\bigl[2(4+3)\\bigr]+4\\times3\\\\204\\times203&=40\\,000+1400+12\\\\204\\times203&=41\\,412\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p6-q14-d-ii",
